[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Close-up of two telescope sensors, one larger and red, the other smaller and black.
AUDIO: 'This is the new ZWO Seestar S50 Pro smart&nbsp; telescope. And I know from the comments on&nbsp;&nbsp; my previous videos that many of you have been&nbsp; eagerly waiting for the release of this scope&nbsp;&nbsp; because on paper it seems like the perfect&nbsp; smart telescope. The reach of the original&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'S50 with all the bells and whistles of the S30&nbsp; Pro, including a larger, more sensitive sensor&nbsp;&nbsp; and improved optics. But on paper specs are one&nbsp; thing. In-person testing reveals a lot more. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'join me as I put this new S50 Pro through&nbsp; its paces and compare it to the original S50&nbsp;&nbsp; and the other Seestar models in a comprehensive&nbsp; review. My name is Nico and this is Nebula Photos.'

[00:38] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A black backpack and a black hard case with the Seestar logo.
AUDIO: 'I\'ve had the Seestar S50 Pro for the past 2&nbsp; months, which has given me time to really get&nbsp;&nbsp; to know it for this review. ZWO provided me with&nbsp; this pre-release unit, but they have no say in&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what I share in this video. So, let\'s jump in.&nbsp; The S50 Pro is the largest and heaviest Seestar&nbsp;&nbsp; release so far. It\'s a full pound heavier than&nbsp; the original S50 and a bit thicker as well. If&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re thinking about airport travel, the S50 Pro&nbsp; case should work as your under the seat carry-on.&nbsp;&nbsp; But of course with the S30 or S30 Pro, I can&nbsp; actually slip the whole thing in its padded&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'case into my under the seat bag where I could also&nbsp; fit in my laptop and other stuff. So the S30 line&nbsp;&nbsp; is still more portable. In terms of storage,&nbsp; the S50 Pro matches the S30 Pro with 128 GB of&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:28] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: The presenter standing next to a white Seestar S50 Pro telescope on a tripod.
AUDIO: 'internal storage, while the non pro Seestars have&nbsp; only 64 GB. Something you\'ll definitely notice&nbsp;&nbsp; with the S50 Pro if you\'ve used the previous&nbsp; Seestars is that it is the quietest yet when&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'slewing and tracking. It\'s whisper quiet. I did&nbsp; a little sound test so you can hear for yourself. I\'ve not taken apart any of the sea stars, but&nbsp; I would guess based on it being quieter that&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the drive system for tracking the sky has been&nbsp; improved in this model. That would also line up&nbsp;&nbsp; with what I observed in terms of tracking ability.&nbsp; With every previous Seestar model, I was typically&nbsp;&nbsp; okay with 30 second exposures in EQ mode, but had&nbsp; issues when I raised that to 60-second exposures.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Not that 60-second exposures wouldn\'t work&nbsp; entirely, but just that the app would reject&nbsp;&nbsp; a lot more frames due to poor tracking. So, then&nbsp; I usually just ended up at 30 seconds to get more&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:25] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: The presenter gesturing while standing next to the Seestar S50 Pro.
AUDIO: 'data through the night. The S50 Pro\'s reject rate&nbsp; with the 60-second exposures is now very low,&nbsp;&nbsp; at least with the sample that I\'ve been testing.&nbsp; Here\'s some data I collected on that, comparing it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the original S50. Another first for the S50&nbsp; Pro is a bigger battery. I believe this is the&nbsp;&nbsp; main reason the Pro is heavier and thicker than&nbsp; the original S50. All three previous seestars&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have 6 amp hour batteries, while the S50 Pro has&nbsp; a 10 amp hour battery. In real world testing, this&nbsp;&nbsp; means the original sea stars get you around four&nbsp; to six hours of run time, while the new S50 Pro&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will go for eight to nine hours on one charge. So,&nbsp; with a battery that lasts that long, I never had&nbsp;&nbsp; to plug in the S50 Pro during use when testing it.&nbsp; Um, because we have short summer nights right now,&nbsp;&nbsp; so it it always lasted through the whole night.&nbsp; However, in the middle of the winter when the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nights are longer and the cold can also shorten uh&nbsp; battery lifespan, it would probably be a different&nbsp;&nbsp; story. The other thing to note is at home, it\'s of&nbsp; course easy to recharge in between sessions with&nbsp;&nbsp; the provided USB cable. But what about when&nbsp; you\'re out on a camping trip visiting a dark&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sky site? How are you supposed to recharge your&nbsp; seestar then? Well, that\'s where Bluetti comes&nbsp;&nbsp; in. Bluetti is sponsoring this video and they make&nbsp; a variety of portable power stations at different&nbsp;&nbsp; sizes and price points. The one that they sent&nbsp; out here is the Elite 100 V2 and I\'ve been testing&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it and it has a very high capacity. Um, it could&nbsp; actually recharge this S50 Pro 20 times over. So,&nbsp;&nbsp; plenty of power even for the most epic of camping&nbsp; trips. In terms of power ports for the Seestar,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:05] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Close-up of the Bluetti Elite 100 V2 power station's display and ports, showing 89% charge.
AUDIO: 'you\'re going to use one of the USB power outputs.&nbsp; It has two USB-A and two USB-C. But the Elite 100&nbsp;&nbsp; V2 also has four AC power outputs and two 12 volt&nbsp; DC center pin uh barrel outputs like we often use&nbsp;&nbsp; with astronomy gear. So plenty of options and&nbsp; enough power that I can power plenty of things&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on my camping trip. For example, I like freshly&nbsp; ground coffee when camping. I know that\'s a little&nbsp;&nbsp; bougie, but there\'s nothing better than really&nbsp; good coffee while out camping in the middle of&nbsp;&nbsp; nowhere. So, I think the combination of the&nbsp; Seestar and a Bluetti portable power station&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'would be perfect for your next dark sky trip. And&nbsp; when you do get all set up on that trip, the first&nbsp;&nbsp; object I\'m guessing many people will observe with&nbsp; their new S50 Pro is the moon if it\'s out. It\'s&nbsp;&nbsp; an easy thing to do with the Seestar. And if&nbsp; we compare the 1x view of the S50 Pro versus&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the original S50, it looks like the original is&nbsp; more magnified. It\'s more zoomed in. But that\'s&nbsp;&nbsp; not actually an accurate way to put it. The moon&nbsp; is taking up the same number of pixels here. It\'s&nbsp;&nbsp; just that the sensor size on the S50 Pro is four&nbsp; times larger than the original S50. We\'re going&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from an HD 1080p sensor on the S50 to a 4K sensor&nbsp; on the S50 Pro. So, that just means there\'s more&nbsp;&nbsp; space around the moon and the moon appears smaller&nbsp; on screen on the S50 Pro, but you\'re actually not&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'losing any actual resolution. If we zoom both into&nbsp; 100%, you can see the same amount of detail. Um,&nbsp;&nbsp; it\'s it\'s they\'re basically identical. And the&nbsp; same thing plays out for all the solar system&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:35] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: The presenter standing next to a white Seestar S50 Pro telescope on a tripod.
AUDIO: 'objects. Basically identical resolution. So here\'s&nbsp; a comparison on the sun and now here is Saturn. So&nbsp;&nbsp; at least in solar system mode, there\'s not much&nbsp; of a difference between what the S50 and the S50&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Pro can see. Milky Way mode, however, is only&nbsp; available on the Seestar Pro models. And it\'s&nbsp;&nbsp; basically identical between the S30 Pro and S50&nbsp; Pro. In both cases, they use a 4K sensor behind a&nbsp;&nbsp; very wide-angle lens that allows for a 63° field&nbsp; of view. You can use it in any part of the sky.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For example, here is the Cygnus constellation&nbsp; captured in Milky Way mode. But something pretty&nbsp;&nbsp; cool it can do if you\'re capturing the the core&nbsp; of the Milky Way is freeze the foreground and&nbsp;&nbsp; blend it back in seamlessly. I\'ve noticed this&nbsp; feature has been improving with each app release,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and so it now lets you spend several minutes on&nbsp; the foreground, which is nice to reduce noise.&nbsp;&nbsp; I also tested the Milky Way arch panorama&nbsp; mode, which can do an 8K resolution uh by&nbsp;&nbsp; expanding the field of view, creating a pano image&nbsp; automatically. It did work here, but it looks a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little bit funky because I was dealing with moving&nbsp; clouds, but it did all stitch correctly. I will&nbsp;&nbsp; have a more detailed look at Seestar\'s Milky Way&nbsp; mode in a video coming out soon, but the main&nbsp;&nbsp; point I want to make here is that the Milky Way&nbsp; features on the S50 Pro are completely identical&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the S30 Pro, and they work quite well.&nbsp; Now, let\'s turn to my favorite, deep sky. Um,&nbsp;&nbsp; and this is what Seestar calls the stargazing&nbsp; mode. This is, I think, the main event of, uh,&nbsp;&nbsp; Seestar, and the S50 Pro is very impressive. Let\'s&nbsp; start by looking at field of view with all four&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sea stars on some popular targets. As you can see&nbsp; here, the S30 Pro has by far the biggest field of&nbsp;&nbsp; view. The S50 Pro is next biggest, and it\'s just a&nbsp; little bit bigger than the standard S30. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:20] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Visual comparison of field of view for Lagoon/Trifid nebula across S30 Pro, S50 Pro, S30, and S50 models.
AUDIO: 'the original S50 has the smallest field of view.&nbsp; I\'m using a telescope simulator here. So, this is&nbsp;&nbsp; uh survey imagery, not images I took with the sea&nbsp; star. I just want to show how various objects fit&nbsp;&nbsp; with each sea star. And the S30 Pro is still going&nbsp; to be your best bet for really big objects. Um,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of course, you can use mosaic mode to expand&nbsp; the smaller FOVs of the sea stars. It just&nbsp;&nbsp; takes longer. I do think the S50 Pro uh at just&nbsp; a little bit bigger than the S30 is a nice middle&nbsp;&nbsp; ground for field of view for many different deep&nbsp; sky objects. So, let\'s take a look at those. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I want to start with a little bit of a technical&nbsp; analysis. So, we\'re going to be looking at&nbsp;&nbsp; basically unprocessed data just stacked. And I&nbsp; have the S50 here on the left and the S50 Pro on&nbsp;&nbsp; the right. And this was taken in alt as mode, 500&nbsp; subs each. So, uh, equal amounts of integration.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:10] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Side-by-side comparison of unprocessed deep sky images from S50 and S50 Pro, showing different fields of view.
AUDIO: 'And with this first comparison, I just wanted&nbsp; to show you again how the field of view changes&nbsp;&nbsp; between the S50 and the S50 Pro. Everything here&nbsp; in black on the S50 is what we\'re gaining with the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'larger sensor of the S50 Pro. Of course, you can&nbsp; always crop in on the S50 Pro image and get just&nbsp;&nbsp; as much detail, if not more, than you get with the&nbsp; S50. So, that\'s the first thing. in terms of uh&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'signal to noise ratio and and just you know that&nbsp; kind of thing. I think maybe we\'re just getting a&nbsp;&nbsp; little bit more signal with the improved sensor&nbsp; of the S50 Pro, but it\'s not a huge difference,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:50] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Zoomed-in comparison of star fields from S50 and S50 Pro, highlighting sharpness differences.
AUDIO: 'of course. Okay, now I\'ve cropped the S50 Pro&nbsp; image into the same size as the S50 because&nbsp;&nbsp; I just want to look at image sharpness. And&nbsp; we\'ll zoom well in here to really look at that.'

[09:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And I think that it\'s it\'s clear that the improved&nbsp; optics, they added an extra element and I guess&nbsp;&nbsp; improved the optics just generally with the S50&nbsp; Pro um has improved image sharpness and we can see&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that through uh the stars, right? There\'s a lot&nbsp; of smaller stars here that are showing in the S50&nbsp;&nbsp; Pro image that are sort of blurred out in the S50&nbsp; image. For instance, this one, this one, this one.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We can just barely see one of those three in the&nbsp; S50 image, while all three are quite clear in&nbsp;&nbsp; the S50 Pro image. And that continues across the&nbsp; field. Even with larger stars, hopefully you can&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:46] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Further zoomed-in comparison of star fields from S50 and S50 Pro, showing tighter stars on the Pro.
AUDIO: 'see they\'re a little bit tighter on the S50 Pro&nbsp; image. But the main thing that you\'re going to&nbsp;&nbsp; see is just that very, very small stars suddenly&nbsp; appear because it\'s a sharper telescope. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it could be the optics. It could be the better&nbsp; tracking on the S50 Pro or it could be probably&nbsp;&nbsp; a combination of both the better tracking and&nbsp; the better optics um that\'s giving us this&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sharper result with the S50 Pro. But I saw this um&nbsp; throughout my testing. This is of course in alt as&nbsp;&nbsp; mode with 10-second exposures, but it was just as&nbsp; pronounced in EQ mode with 60-second exposures how&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:26] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Highly saturated images from S50 and S50 Pro to compare color correction and chromatic aberration.
AUDIO: 'much sharper the S50 Pro was uh compared to the&nbsp; S50. So that\'s the sharpness comparison. Okay.&nbsp;&nbsp; Now this is the color correction comparison. These&nbsp; are both highly saturated images to show you the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'color correction. Basically what I\'m seeing&nbsp; here is that uh the original S50 which is a&nbsp;&nbsp; triplet design is going more to the green and how&nbsp; it\'s uh not perfectly color corrected and the S50&nbsp;&nbsp; Pro is going more towards the magenta side. Now&nbsp; both of these it\'s not a big issue in processing.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can definitely deal with this. But this is&nbsp; what we call chromatic aberration. And the S50,&nbsp;&nbsp; it\'s on the green side. On the S50 Pro, it\'s on&nbsp; the magenta side. Okay, that\'s all I\'m going to&nbsp;&nbsp; show in terms of comparisons between the S50&nbsp; and the S50 Pro for deep sky objects. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m just going to show you my processed results&nbsp; that I got with the S50 Pro and talk about these&nbsp;&nbsp; just a little bit. So, this I think is my favorite&nbsp; image I\'ve ever taken with a smart scope. Um,&nbsp;&nbsp; it\'s just about 6 hours of various exposure&nbsp; lengths, some 10 seconds, some 30 seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some 60 seconds, all combined together and cropped&nbsp; in on the Iris. And I don\'t know, I just think it&nbsp;&nbsp; really turned out to my liking really well. Um,&nbsp; there\'s really nice detail in here on the center&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the the reflection nebula on Iris. But then&nbsp; as a whole, I just really like how I was able to&nbsp;&nbsp; bring out the colorful star field and this rich&nbsp; chocolaty dark dust all around the iris. Um,&nbsp;&nbsp; so I just think it turned out really well and uh&nbsp; the S50 Pro definitely impressed me with uh what&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it could do on a sort of difficult target like&nbsp; this, the Iris Nebula. Now here\'s a less difficult&nbsp;&nbsp; target. This is often, you know, one of the first&nbsp; of three targets that people go after along with&nbsp;&nbsp; Orion and the Pleiades. This is, of course, the&nbsp; Andromeda Galaxy Messier 31, and I only spent 30&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'minutes on it. Uh, which I think for 30 minutes,&nbsp; this is very impressive. If I tried 30 minutes&nbsp;&nbsp; with just like a a DSLR untracked, I could not&nbsp; reach this kind of result. It\'s definitely a very&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh nice result. For just 30 minutes, I was able to&nbsp; bring out some of the hot blue stars in the outer&nbsp;&nbsp; arms. Uh, lots of nice detail here in the core&nbsp; area and the dust lanes. And overall, I\'d be uh&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pretty happy with this. I said there would be no&nbsp; more comparisons, but I Let me show you one more&nbsp;&nbsp; comparison here. This is not between the S50 and&nbsp; S50 Pro, but just between what the S50 Pro creates&nbsp;&nbsp; if you do no processing. If you just accept the&nbsp; default stack of 2 hours on the Cygnus wall region&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:59] [scene:result]
VISUAL: A processed image of the Cygnus Wall region in the North America Nebula taken with the S50 Pro.
AUDIO: 'in the North America Nebula versus 2 hours that&nbsp; I\'ve processed with my processing technique of&nbsp;&nbsp; um sort of accentuating the O3 signal and and&nbsp; and making more of a color separation. This&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was of course done with the light pollution filter&nbsp; which is unchanged from previous sea star models.&nbsp;&nbsp; It\'s a wide uh band pass dual narrowband filter.&nbsp; I think it\'s like 20 and 30 nanometer band passes&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or something like that. So they call it a light&nbsp; pollution filter. You could also call it a dual&nbsp;&nbsp; narrow band filter. Okay. And then the last one&nbsp; to show you here. This is a challenging object.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I wanted to go after one really challenging&nbsp; narrowband object with the S50 Pro to see&nbsp;&nbsp; what it could do on something quite dim. This is&nbsp; Sharpless 132, sometimes called the Lion Nebula in&nbsp;&nbsp; Cepheus. And it\'s a really interesting nebula.&nbsp; It has a lot of Ha signal, a lot of O3 signal,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but the O3 signal is is very dim. So, it does&nbsp; take um some integration to bring it out. This is&nbsp;&nbsp; 13 hours on the Lion Nebula. And if we look here&nbsp; in the center, it did a good job on bringing out&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this O3 river. This is sort of an interesting&nbsp; little part of it right here. It also brought&nbsp;&nbsp; out a lot of background Ha, you know, like this.&nbsp; All this stuff down here is quite dim. And it it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shows how sensitive this this sensor is that it it&nbsp; was able to do this much on such a faint emission&nbsp;&nbsp; nebula. One thing I haven\'t talked about yet is&nbsp; price. And the pre-order price for the S50 Pro is&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:34] [scene:pricing]
VISUAL: A visual comparison of Seestar S30 ($399), S30 Pro ($699), and S50 Pro ($999) models with their prices.
AUDIO: '$899. After pre-order sales end, it will retail&nbsp; for $999. For context, the S30 Pro is $699 and&nbsp;&nbsp; the normal S30 is $399. The original S50 has been&nbsp; discontinued for a while now, several months, but&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'its last price before being discontinued was $549.&nbsp; Now, if we just look at the Seestars that will be&nbsp;&nbsp; available going forward, the S30 is obviously the&nbsp; value model competing with the Dwarf Mini on the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'low end with fewer frills, no secondary camera.&nbsp; The S30 Pro and S50 Pro are both more feature-&nbsp;&nbsp; packed with the secondary camera, which can do&nbsp; Milky Way mode, star trails, and other things.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And but the Pro models also have the bigger more&nbsp; sensitive sensor and that\'s going to make a big&nbsp;&nbsp; difference for stargazing mode especially on the&nbsp; dimmer deep sky objects that I like like nebulae.&nbsp;&nbsp; Between the two Pro models, the main benefit&nbsp; of the S30 Pro is going to be portability. It\'s&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:29] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: The presenter standing next to the Seestar S50 Pro, concluding the review.
AUDIO: 'definitely easier to take everywhere at about&nbsp; half the size and half the weight. And for me,&nbsp;&nbsp; the main benefit of the S50 Pro is the better&nbsp; tracking. But for others, it might be the&nbsp;&nbsp; increased resolution for going after smaller&nbsp; objects like small galaxies. Many things in&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Messier catalog are actually pretty small. In&nbsp; any case, with each smart telescope release, I do&nbsp;&nbsp; see them catching up and getting closer and closer&nbsp; to the results that I can get with an entry-level&nbsp;&nbsp; traditional astrophotography rig. So, I\'m curious&nbsp; to see what we will get next out of the Seestar&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'line. But I can confidently say the S50 Pro is&nbsp; the best Seestar that ZWO has made to date. So&nbsp;&nbsp; that\'s it for this one. Till next time, this has&nbsp; been Nico Carver wishing everyone clear skies.'
